WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:07.040
Your goal is simple: eliminate all disease. The constraint is also simple: resources are scarce.

2
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:14.300
You have limited money, time, and talent. Every dollar on a low-impact trial is a dollar not curing cancer.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:19.060
Every researcher writing grant applications is a researcher not doing research.

4
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:25.120
Every patient excluded from trials is data you'll never collect and a person you'll never help.

5
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.720
Your current system fails this optimization so thoroughly it almost looks intentional.

6
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:38.440
Your N.I.H. spends forty-seven billion dollars a year. Your scientists spend fifty to sixty-seven percent of their time

7
00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:44.860
writing grant applications instead of doing science. Billions flow to projects that never produce treatments.

8
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.100
Your system isn't designed to maximize cures. It's designed to maximize grant-writing.

9
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:57.420
This isn't a conspiracy. It's just what happens when you design a system that rewards asking for money instead of producing

10
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:04.760
results. You've accidentally built a machine whose primary output is paperwork and whose secondary output is occasionally,

11
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:13.080
reluctantly, medicine. Your Decentralized Institute of Health (D.I.H.) is an alternative design that optimizes for a single

12
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:20.460
metric: maximum R.O.I. toward disease eradication. It's what you'd build if you started from scratch and actually wanted

13
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:25.400
results. The Health-Industrial Complex: Coordinating Your War on Disease.

14
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:34.080
The Olsonian Problem. Your economist Mancur Olson identified why public goods are systematically underproduced: diffuse

15
00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:41.680
benefits and concentrated costs. In simpler terms: everyone benefits from a cure for cancer, but nobody benefits enough

16
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:45.380
to fight for it the way a weapons manufacturer fights for a bomber contract.

17
00:01:46.420 --> 00:02:51.020
It's one of those observations that's obvious once someone says it, and invisible before, which describes most of your species'

18
00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:03.900
problems. Curing cancer benefits eight billion people a little.

19
00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.840
But nobody benefits enough to organize, lobby, and fight for it the way a defense contractor fights for a bomber contract.

20
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:54.880
The eight billion don't show up. This is why your species has spent fifty years "fighting cancer" while your defense industry

21
00:04:54.880 --> 00:09:37.420
got stealth bombers, aircraft carriers, and G.P.S. Your military-industrial complex solved this problem for defense.

22
00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:42.020
Defense contractors, generals, politicians, and workers all have concentrated interests in military spending.

23
00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:42.300
They coordinate. They lobby. They win budgets. Result: the most powerful military in human history.

24
00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:47.320
Disease has no such coalition. Your patients are too sick to lobby.

25
00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:55.340
Your researchers compete for scraps. Your funders lack coordination.

26
00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.560
Your politicians get no credit for cures that arrive after their term ends (which is all of them, because cures take fifteen

27
00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:16.900
years and terms last four). Everyone wants disease eradicated; no one has a concentrated interest in making it happen.

28
00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.140
It's like everyone wanting a clean kitchen but nobody wanting to do the dishes, except the dishes are cancer and the kitchen

29
00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:26.460
is on fire. Your Decentralized Institutes of Health, or D.I.H., solves the Olsonian problem by creating concentrated interests

30
00:10:26.460 --> 00:11:22.680
in disease eradication. Your military-industrial complex coordinates actors around defense.

31
00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:28.600
Your D.I.H. creates a health-industrial complex that coordinates actors around eradication.

32
00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:45.700
Same structure, opposite purpose. Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, or S.H.A.E.F., for Your War on Disease.

33
00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:48.460
In nineteen forty-four, Eisenhower didn't replace the Allied armies.

34
00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:48.460
He coordinated them. Set the objective, allocated resources, made sure everyone pulled in the same direction.

35
00:11:48.460 --> 00:13:01.600
Your D.I.H. is S.H.A.E.F. for the war on disease. Pharma companies stay pharma companies.

36
00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:01.620
Universities stay universities. Patient groups stay patient groups.

37
00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:30.040
But they operate as one force because your D.I.H. coordinates them toward eradication.

38
00:13:31.920 --> 00:14:04.800
Your war on disease has been losing for fifty years because it's not actually a war.

39
00:14:10.120 --> 00:14:13.740
It's a collection of uncoordinated skirmishes where researchers compete for grants, pharma companies hide failures, and

40
00:14:13.740 --> 00:15:21.240
patients can't access trials. You've been "fighting" cancer the way a cat "fights" a laser pointer: lots of energy, no coordination,

41
00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:26.380
nothing caught. Imagine if D-Day had been run this way. The Americans land at one beach, the British at another.

42
00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:26.380
Neither tells the other what they learned. Both compete for the same supply ships while the enemy reads their grant applications.

43
00:16:22.740 --> 00:17:42.260
Your D.I.H. is the coordination layer that turns scattered skirmishes into a unified campaign.

44
00:17:43.040 --> 00:18:30.740
Your D.I.H.: The return on investment, or R.O.I. Maximization Protocol.

45
00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:44.100
Your D.I.H. is not a platform. It's not an organization. It's not even really a thing.

46
00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:58.880
It's a coordination protocol with one function: Make every actor do the highest-R.O.I.

47
00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:03.300
thing toward total disease eradication. This means: Every dollar flows to maximum impact.

48
00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:07.620
Every researcher works on highest-value problems. Every patient joins trials that matter most.

49
00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:13.020
Nothing gets wasted on low-R.O.I. activities. On your planet, you call this radical.

50
00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.740
On mine, we call it "obvious." Three Core Functions. Your D.I.H.

51
00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.480
does exactly three three things: One. Receive funds (from the one percent Treaty, donations, etc.).

52
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Two. Allocate research via patient subsidies (market mechanism) and infrastructure via Wishocracy.

53
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Three. Verify results and pay proportional to impact. Everything operational is outsourced.

54
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Trial infrastructure? Existing and new providers compete for that work.

55
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Task decomposition? A.I. services. Talent matching? Existing marketplaces.

56
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Crowdfunding? Existing platforms. Why stay thin? Because thin protocols are hard to capture.

57
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
There's nothing to bribe. No operational role to corrupt. No C.E.O.

58
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
to take on a yacht trip. Just code that moves money toward measured outcomes.

59
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your species has a habit of corrupting every institution you build.

60
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
You corrupt them the way water corrodes pipes: inevitably, given enough time and contact.

61
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
This one is boring enough that nobody bothers. The Core Innovation: Pay for Results.

62
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Every dollar flows based on results, not promises: Patients vote with their enrollment, meaning researchers get paid for

63
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
attracting patients. Outcomes determine continued funding, meaning campaigns that deliver get more and failures get defunded.

64
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
No one gets paid for writing grant proposals. No one gets paid for attending review committees.

65
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
No one gets paid for publishing papers about why their research might work someday.

66
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
This is how most other industries work. You pay contractors when they build the house, not when they promise to build it.

67
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
You pay farmers when they grow the food, not when they apply for a farming license.

68
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your Data Commons: Publish Everything. Your current system hides failures.

69
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Companies bury negative results. Researchers don't publish what didn't work.

70
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your scientists waste billions repeating mistakes someone else already made because they literally cannot find out those

71
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
mistakes were already made. It's like your whole species has amnesia, but only for the embarrassing parts.

72
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
You remember your triumphs in high definition and forget your failures completely.

73
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
This is called "being human." It's also called "why you keep dying."

74
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your D.I.H. requires one hundred percent open publication of all data, positive and negative, as a condition of funding:

75
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Every trial, every result, and every dataset is published. A.I.

76
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
models scan the global data commons, finding patterns humans miss.

77
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Failed experiments become shared knowledge, not repeated waste.

78
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
This is intelligence sharing in the Supreme Headquarters Allied Expeditionary Force, or S.H.A.E.F., analogy.

79
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your Allies won partly because they shared Ultra intercepts across commands.

80
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your war on disease loses because everyone guards their failures like trade secrets.

81
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Think of it as a group chat where everyone shares what didn't work.

82
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Except the group is your entire species and the topic is death prevention.

83
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Governance: Market Mechanism and Wishocracy. Your D.I.H. uses two allocation mechanisms: Under patient subsidies, a market

84
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
mechanism, research funding follows patient choice. Patients enroll in trials they believe in, and funding flows accordingly.

85
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Variable subsidy rates ensure rare diseases remain viable. No committees decide cancer versus Alzheimer's.

86
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Wishocracy is used for infrastructure and public goods only.

87
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It aggregates preferences through pairwise comparisons (electronic health record, or E.H.R., integration or security audits?).

88
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
The Incentive Stack: Making R.O.I. the Selfish Choice. Your D.I.H.

89
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
doesn't rely on altruism. Altruism is lovely but flaky. It shows up when it feels like it, cancels last minute, and is always

90
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
"busy that weekend." Instead, your D.I.H. pays everyone to do the highest-R.O.I.

91
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
thing. Greed is more dependable than kindness. Your entire economic system proves this daily.

92
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Nobody ever forgot to be greedy. Researchers seek money and prestige; they receive per-patient subsidies that are higher

93
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
for rare diseases, so attracting patients means getting paid.

94
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Patients seek health and compensation; subsidies scale with trial importance, so joining high-priority trials means getting

95
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
paid more. Politicians seek re-election and legacy; Incentive Alignment Bonds (I.A.B.s) are tied to disease outcomes, so

96
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
better outcomes mean a bigger bonus. Funders seek impact and returns; quadratic matching and outcome tracking ensure high-R.O.I.

97
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
donations have an amplified impact. Data providers seek revenue; fees are tied to data utility, so more useful data leads

98
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
to more revenue. Campaigns seek funding; results-based continued funding means a higher R.O.I.

99
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
leads to more funding. Every incentive is R.O.I.-weighted. Not just "do good" but "do the MOST good per dollar per hour."

100
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your D.I.H. makes the highest-R.O.I. action the selfish choice for every actor.

101
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Selfishness, properly directed, cures cancer. How Your Researchers Get Paid.

102
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your traditional system: Write a grant proposal. Hope a committee likes it.

103
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Get paid to try. Maybe produce results. Maybe not. Get paid either way.

104
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your D.I.H. system: One. Per-patient subsidies: The more patients who join your trial, the more funding you get.

105
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Patients vote with their enrollment. Two. Variable subsidy rates: Rare diseases get higher per-patient subsidies.

106
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Small patient pools don't mean small budgets. Three. Results-based continuation: Deliver results, get more funding.

107
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Don't deliver, get defunded. Like every other job on your planet except, somehow, research.

108
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Pay your scientists like you pay your plumbers: for fixing the problem, not for explaining why it's hard.

109
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
How It Works For Your Patients. Your D.I.H. doesn't replace your healthcare system.

110
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It adds an "experimental treatment insurance layer" on top.

111
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your species already understands insurance. You just hadn't thought to apply it to not dying of untreated diseases.

112
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Sarah has Type two Diabetes. Old system: Metformin not working.

113
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
No other options covered. Pays hundreds per month for a branded drug.

114
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Still has poor control. With your D.I.H.: Her doctor checks the trial network during a regular visit.

115
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Sees relevant trials. Recommends one based on Sarah's profile.

116
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Sarah enrolls with one click. The trial coverage pool covers all costs.

117
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Sarah pays a small copay. Gets an experimental drug that might work better.

118
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Reports blood sugar via app. Her data helps the next patient.

119
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Total time added to doctor visit: three minutes. Why your doctors will cooperate: More treatment options for desperate patients.

120
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
No liability (covered by trial insurance). Minimal workflow changes.

121
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Why your patients will use it: Affordable access to experimental treatments.

122
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Doctor-recommended. Insurance-like coverage (familiar model).

123
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
No financial risk. They help others while helping themselves.

124
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It's like being a guinea pig, except the guinea pig gets paid and gets medicine.

125
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
How the Money Flows. The Architecture. The one percent Treaty Fund: It holds the treasury (from the one percent Treaty).

126
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It allocates between infrastructure and public goods via Wishocracy.

127
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It funds campaigns, not bureaucracies. No C.E.O., no board, no one to corrupt.

128
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your Decentralized Institute of Health (D.I.H.): A thin coordination protocol.

129
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Receives funding from the one percent Treaty Fund. Research allocation via patient subsidies (market mechanism).

130
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Verifies results, pays for outcomes. Trial infrastructure providers: Funded campaigns that compete to provide trial infrastructure.

131
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Not part of your D.I.H. itself; just service providers with no budget authority.

132
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
The Fund Flow. Your one percent Treaty redirects twenty-seven point two billion dollars a year from global military budgets

133
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
into the one percent Treaty Fund. But not all of it reaches research (some of it goes to keeping the machine running): Incentive

134
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Alignment Bonds receive ten percent plus ten percent, or two point seven two billion plus two point seven two billion dollars,

135
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
to align investors and politicians with outcomes. The Research treasury receives eighty percent, totaling twenty-one point

136
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
eight billion dollars, for patient subsidies via market allocation.

137
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
What Gets Funded: Market Failures Only. Most research allocation happens automatically (patients choose trials, funding

138
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
flows there). Your one percent Treaty Fund primarily funds market failures.

139
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
These are the things your ecosystem can't handle on its own.

140
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Infrastructure Development and operations. Competing alternative implementations.

141
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Data commons infrastructure (storage, processing). Security audits and fraud detection systems.

142
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
True Public Goods (No Revenue Model) Patient trial participation subsidies.

143
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Negative results publishing. Replication studies. This is minimal by design.

144
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your ecosystem handles most research funding automatically: companies register treatments, patients join trials, revenue

145
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
flows, research happens. Your D.I.H. only directs the one percent Treaty Fund to cover what the market genuinely can't.

146
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
What Your D.I.H. Outsources (and Why). Your D.I.H. is intentionally minimal.

147
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It outsources everything operational because the best way to avoid corruption is to have nothing worth corrupting.

148
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
If a provider stops performing, you fund a better one. This is how your species runs restaurants.

149
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Time to try it with medicine. Trial infrastructure is outsourced to competing providers through funded campaigns to separate

150
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
concerns. Crowdfunding is outsourced to existing platforms because they already exist and work.

151
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Talent matching is outsourced to existing marketplaces so you don't reinvent what already works.

152
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Data storage is outsourced to competing providers to encourage market competition.

153
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Every outsourced function follows the same lifecycle: propose, vote via Wishocracy, receive funding, deliver, get measured,

154
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
and earn continued funding based on results. Anti-Capture Design.

155
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your current system is trivially captured. Concentrate billions of dollars in a few committees, and lobbyists will find

156
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
them. This is as predictable as gravity, and your species keeps being surprised by it the way a dog is surprised by a vacuum

157
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
cleaner. Every single time. Your D.I.H. makes capture economically irrational.

158
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
How Your D.I.H. Resists Capture. With no C.E.O., there is nothing to bribe, making capture costs not applicable.

159
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Algorithmic governance uses rules encoded in transparent, auditable systems because you can't bribe an algorithm.

160
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
A public ledger tracks every dollar so corruption is visible.

161
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
The system is replaceable because anyone can build a competing alternative, and capture triggers competition.

162
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Distributed voting allows millions to vote via Wishocracy, ensuring lobbying doesn't scale.

163
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Outcome-based funding ensures results determine allocation, making gaming harder than performing.

164
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your protocol ensures no single component can monopolize power.

165
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Everything is replaceable. Nothing is essential except the coordination rules themselves.

166
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
If any campaign provider gets captured, fund a competing one instead.

167
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Security: Defense in Depth. A twenty-seven point two billion dollar treasury is a massive target.

168
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Every thief, hacker, and corrupt bureaucrat on your planet will try to steal from it.

169
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
This is not speculation. This is a certainty, like sunrise or a politician lying.

170
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Your D.I.H. uses defense in depth: Distributed control ensures no single person or committee holds the keys.

171
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Community governance uses time-delayed execution so fraud can be caught before funds move.

172
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Automated fraud detection provides real-time anomaly detection, duplication monitoring, and whistleblower bounties.

173
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Machines watch the money so humans don't have to be trusted with it.

174
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Full transparency means every dollar is tracked on public ledgers.

175
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Regular independent audits are conducted. You can't steal what everyone can see.

176
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Recovery mechanisms include clawbacks for data falsification.

177
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Emergency pause capabilities are built-in. If something goes wrong, the system stops and fixes itself before continuing.

178
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Summary: Your Coordination Layer. Your D.I.H. is not a research institution, a trial platform, or a funding agency.

179
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
It's the thin layer that coordinates all of them. The one percent Treaty Fund serves as the treasury and receives funds

180
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
from the treaty. Patient subsidies handle research allocation through a market mechanism (patients choose trials).

181
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Wishocracy manages infrastructure governance and allocates between infrastructure and public goods.

182
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Trial infrastructure provides clinical trial services through funded campaigns with competing providers.

183
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
Incentive Alignment Bonds ensure investor and political alignment through outcome-based rewards.

184
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
The single sentence version: Your D.I.H. receives funds, allocates research via patient subsidies, governs infrastructure

185
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
via Wishocracy, verifies results, and pays proportional to outcomes, making the highest R.O.I.

186
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
action the selfish choice for every actor. That's the theory.

187
00:19:21.480 --> 00:19:21.480
The rest of this manual explains how you actually build it.
